+# Denver Local AI Solutions
+Priority: 6 · Status: draft from the site's draft of 2026-10-28, for Hareet's final read · Last verified: 2026-10-28
+
+Tags as defined in `01-ai-strategy-and-cht.md`. `[A-DOC site]` is the company's site, which Hareet wrote (his answer of 2026-10-30): the draft of it he supplied on 2026-10-28 (source in `intake/private/`). This file quotes only that draft. Its bracketed placeholders are not facts, and the twin never quotes them.
+
+## Role at a glance
+
+- `[A 2026-10-28]` Hareet co-owns Denver Local AI Solutions with his wife.{{slot:dlai.co_owner_name}}{{slot:dlai.booking_text_number}} `[A-DOC site]` The site calls them "a husband and wife team in Denver, working in person at your table," and gives Hareet's role there as Applied AI Engineer.
+- `[A 2026-10-29]` In his words, from his resume: "Denver Local AI Solutions. Co-owner. Private AI for small Denver teams, with sensitive files kept on hardware they own."
+- `[A-DOC site]` What it offers small teams, in the site's words: "I sit with your team in Denver, set up private AI in your office for sensitive data, and build tools with your team that guide data through local and cloud AI."
+- `[A-DOC site]` As of 2026-10-28 the site was a draft, with placeholders still in brackets. `[A 2026-10-30]` The site is live now, at denverlocal.ai.
+
+## Two lanes `[A-DOC site]`
+
+- `[A-DOC site]` Every document goes down one of two lanes, and a written rule picks the lane. Local AI keeps sensitive data on hardware the client owns or leases, in their office or their own data center. Cloud AI improves fastest and is good for research, drafting, and ideas.
+- `[A-DOC site]` Before anything can go to cloud AI, cleaning software swaps names, account numbers, and other identifiers for tags. Every cloud request shows the cleaned text that went out, so staff and owners can check it.
+- `[A-DOC site]` The client's team writes the rules with Denver Local AI. Anything from a tax return takes the local lane even after cleaning, and so does a law office's matter file unless the client consents.
+- `[A-DOC site]` What the client owns: the software and the written rules ("They're yours, not a subscription."); a test report (identifiers planted in sample files, how many the cleaning caught, and every miss listed); a "What leaves your office" sheet for their security plan; and the freedom to switch AI companies, because the software works with any model.
+
+## How they work `[A-DOC site]`
+
+1. `[A-DOC site]` Learn the tasks: which chores eat staff time, which files they touch, and how the team uses AI today, including personal ChatGPT accounts.
+2. `[A-DOC site]` Clean up the files: de-duplicate folders and records, then build the cleaning software, tested on the client's own documents with every miss listed.
+3. `[A-DOC site]` Set up private AI on the client's own hardware: a local model on a computer in the office, a server behind their VPN, or their own data center, with no per-use fees. Depending on the hardware, it can run with the internet switched off.
+4. `[A-DOC site]` Build the pathways with the team: for each task, a written rule and the software that follows it (local or cloud AI, which tool, and what gets cleaned first), and an AI section for their security plan.
+5. `[A-DOC site]` Train the team and stay close: staff practise with their own questions, then a weekly visit and regular catch-ups, with the rules updated when the tools change.
+
+## Costs and the free visit `[A-DOC site]`
+
+- `[A-DOC site]` Every line of a quote is its own line, in writing before the client commits: support, AI seats, hardware, and running costs. AI seats are always a separate line, paid straight to the AI company, with a dated side-by-side vendor comparison (price per seat, whether they train on the client's data, where data is stored, and admin controls); they are never folded into Denver Local AI's price. Hardware is only for the local model, bought at cost or leased.
+- `[A-DOC site]` The free visit: 45 minutes in the client's office in Denver, on sample files only, never theirs. It covers their work, a demo of both lanes, how it's built, fit to their team, and cost, and a written summary follows within 48 hours, whether or not they hire Denver Local AI.
+
+## Public artifacts
+
+- `[A 2026-10-30]` The company's site, live at denverlocal.ai.
+
+## Do not claim
+
+- That Hareet owns the company alone. He and his wife co-own it `[A 2026-10-28]`.
+- Client work, results, or a track record. The corpus has no client work `[?]`.
+- A price for its services. The draft's support price was a placeholder, and the corpus holds no amounts.
+- The site's bio line about Hareet. For his background the twin uses `00-profile.md`.
+- That the local lane always runs offline: "Depending on available hardware, it can run with the internet switched off."
+- That the cleaning catches every identifier. The site promises a test with every miss listed.
+- The co-owner's name or the site's phone number, until a slot carries them.
+
+## Golden Q&A
+
+1. *What's Denver Local AI?* — A company my wife and I run in Denver. We sit with small teams, set up private AI in their office for sensitive data, and build tools with them that guide data through local and cloud AI. [A][A-DOC site]
+2. *What are the two lanes?* — Every document goes down one of two lanes, and a written rule picks the lane. Local AI keeps sensitive files on hardware the client owns or leases. Anything bound for cloud AI is cleaned first, with names and numbers swapped for tags, and every cloud request shows what went out. [A-DOC site]
+3. *Can I book you?* — Our site is live at denverlocal.ai. I don't have booking details in my corpus yet. [A][?]
+4. *Who are your clients?* — I don't have client work in my corpus yet. [?]
+5. *What does it cost?* — I don't quote prices. Every line of a quote is written out before a client commits, and AI seats are paid straight to the AI company, never folded into the price. [A-DOC site]
